{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1789824402841,"sessionID":"ses_f4629b1bbffezZEiat5kIdVPT6","part":{"partID":"prt_0b9d904e9001sJOGt26Svjafaw","sessionID":"ses_f4629b1bbffezZEiat5kIdVPT6","messageID":"msg_0b9d8fc72001auYg2KZUv447mT","type":"tool","id":"call_00_9poKWoHOMIc8xfSOw5Kh2122","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"limit":280,"offset":735,"path":"/tmp/opencode/filing_lines.txt"},"output":"Read file /tmp/opencode/filing_lines.txt, lines 735-1014\n735: Annex D\n736: and incorporated into this proxy statement by reference. Failure to timely comply with the procedures set forth in Section 7-1.2-1202 of the RIBCA\n737: will result in loss of a preferred shareholder’s dissenters’ rights.\n738: Market Price of Our Common Stock\n739: (page 71)\n740: Our common stock is listed on the NASDAQ under the trading symbol “PWX”. The closing sale price of Company common stock on\n741: the NASDAQ on August 12, 2016, which was the last trading day before we announced the merger, was $16.30. On September 16, 2016, the last trading day before the date of this proxy statement, the closing price of Company common stock on the NASDAQ\n742: was $24.88.\n743: 11\n744: Table of Contents\n745: Material Federal Income Tax Consequences\n746: (page 74)\n747: The receipt of cash for shares of Company stock pursuant to the merger generally will be a taxable transaction for United States federal income\n748: tax purposes. In general, if you are a U.S. Holder (as defined under “Material United States Federal Income Tax Consequences of the Merger”), you will recognize gain or loss for United States federal income tax purposes in an amount equal\n749: to the difference between the amount of cash received and your adjusted tax basis in your shares of common stock or preferred stock, as applicable. Generally, any gain recognized upon the exchange will be capital gain, and any such capital gain will\n750: be long-term capital gain if you have established a holding period of more than one year for your shares of Company stock.\n751: Please review\n752: the information under “Material United States Federal Income Tax Consequences of the Merger.” Tax matters are complicated, and the tax consequences of the merger to you will depend upon the facts of your particular situation. In addition,\n753: you may be subject to state, local or foreign tax laws that are not discussed in this document.\n754: Accordingly, we strongly urge you to consult your own tax advisor for a full understanding of the tax consequences to you of the merger.\n755: 12\n756: Table of Contents\n757: QUESTIONS AND ANSWERS ABOUT THE MERGER AND THE SPECIAL MEETING\n758: The following questions and answers are intended to address briefly some commonly asked questions regarding the merger and the\n759: special meeting. These questions and answers may not address all questions that may be important to you as a shareholder. To more fully understand the merger and the special meeting, you should read this entire proxy statement, including the\n760: materials attached as annexes, as well as the documents that have been incorporated by reference into this proxy statement.\n761: Q:\n762: Why am I receiving this proxy statement?\n763: A:\n764: G&W and the Company have agreed to the acquisition of the Company by G&W under the terms of a merger agreement that is described in this proxy statement. A copy of the merger agreement is attached to this proxy\n765: statement as\n766: Annex A\n767: . In order to complete the merger, the holders of a majority of preferred stock and the holders of a majority of common stock, each voting as a separate class, must approve the merger agreement. The Company will hold a\n768: special meeting of its shareholders to obtain these approvals. You are receiving this proxy statement in connection with the solicitation by the Board of proxies of the Company’s shareholders in favor of the merger proposal.\n769: You are also being asked to vote on a proposal to approve on an advisory (non-binding) basis the compensation that may be paid or become\n770: payable by the Company to the Company’s named executive officers in connection with, or following the completion of, the merger and on a proposal to approve an adjournment of the special meeting, if necessary, to permit further solicitation of\n771: proxies if there are not sufficient votes at the time of the special meeting to approve the merger agreement.\n772: This proxy statement\n773: contains important information about the merger, the merger agreement, the special meeting of the Company’s shareholders, and other related matters, and you should read it carefully. The enclosed voting materials for the special meeting allow\n774: you to vote your shares of preferred stock and/or shares of common stock without attending the special meeting. Your vote is very important. Even if you plan to attend the special meeting, we encourage you to submit a proxy as soon as possible.\n775: Q:\n776: What will happen in the merger?\n777: A:\n778: In the proposed merger, Merger Sub will merge with and into the Company, with the Company being the surviving corporation and becoming a wholly-owned subsidiary of G&W.\n779: Q:\n780: What will I receive in the merger?\n781: A:\n782: If the merger agreement is approved and the merger is subsequently completed, you will be entitled to receive $25.00 in cash, without interest, less any applicable withholding taxes, for each share of common stock you\n783: own. If the merger is completed, each outstanding share of preferred stock will be deemed to be automatically converted based upon its existing 100-for-1 conversion ratio, along with the aggregate accrued or accumulated and unpaid dividends thereon,\n784: into 100 shares of common stock, and each holder of preferred stock will be entitled to receive $25.00 in cash for each share of common stock into which the preferred stock has been deemed converted, without interest, less any applicable withholding\n785: taxes.\n786: For example, if you own 100 shares of common stock you will receive $2,500 in cash for your shares of common stock,\n787: less any applicable withholding taxes, and if you own 10 shares of preferred stock, your 10 shares of preferred stock will be deemed converted into 1,000 shares of common stock and you will receive $25,000 in cash in for your shares of preferred\n788: stock deemed converted into common stock, less any applicable withholding taxes. You will not own any shares of the capital stock in the surviving corporation.\n789: Q:\n790: What other effects will the merger have on the Company?\n791: A:\n792: If the merger is completed, the common stock will be delisted from the NASDAQ and deregistered under the Exchange\n793: Act, and the Company will no longer be required to file periodic reports with the SEC with\n794: 13\n795: Table of Contents\n796: respect to the common stock, in each case, in accordance with applicable law, rules and regulations. Following the completion of the merger, the common stock will no longer be publicly traded and\n797: you will no longer have any interest in the Company’s future earnings or growth.\n798: Q:\n799: What are the material federal income tax consequences of the merger to me?\n800: A:\n801: The receipt of cash for shares of Company stock pursuant to the merger generally will be a taxable transaction for United States federal income tax purposes. In general, if you are a U.S. Holder (as defined under\n802: “Material United States Federal Income Tax Consequences of the Merger”), you will recognize gain or loss for United States federal income tax purposes in an amount equal to the difference between the amount of cash received and your\n803: adjusted tax basis in your shares of common stock or preferred stock, as applicable. Generally, any gain recognized upon the exchange will be capital gain, and any such capital gain will be long-term capital gain if you have established a holding\n804: period of more than one year for your shares of Company stock.\n805: This tax treatment may not apply to all the Company’s\n806: shareholders. We strongly urge you to review the information under “Material United States Federal Income Tax Consequences of the Merger” and consult your own tax advisor for a full understanding of the tax consequences of the merger to\n807: you.\n808: Q:\n809: What are the conditions to completion of the merger?\n810: A:\n811: The obligations of G&W and the Company to complete the merger are subject to the satisfaction or waiver of certain closing conditions contained in the merger agreement, including the receipt of required regulatory\n812: approvals and approval of the merger agreement by a majority of the holders of preferred stock and the holders of common stock, each voting as a separate class.\n813: Q:\n814: When do you expect the merger to be completed?\n815: A:\n816: We will complete the merger when all of the conditions to completion contained in the merger agreement are satisfied or waived. Some of these conditions, such as the receipt of required regulatory approvals, are not\n817: entirely within our control. We currently expect to complete the merger during the fourth calendar quarter of 2016 assuming timely satisfaction of necessary closing conditions; however, because the merger is subject to these conditions, we cannot\n818: predict the actual timing and we cannot guarantee that the merger will be completed.\n819: Q:\n820: When and where is the special meeting?\n821: A:\n822: The special meeting of shareholders of the Company will be held at Union Station located at Two Washington Square, Worcester, Massachusetts 01604, on Wednesday, October 26, 2016 at 10:00 a.m., local time.\n823: Q:\n824: What will happen at the special meeting?\n825: A:\n826: At the special meeting, the Company’s shareholders will consider and vote upon a proposal to approve the merger agreement and the non-binding compensation proposal. If, at the time of the special meeting, there are\n827: not sufficient votes to approve the merger agreement, we may ask you to consider and vote upon the adjournment proposal, so that we can solicit additional proxies. Shareholders may also be asked to consider and act upon such other matters as may\n828: properly come before the special meeting or any adjournment or postponement thereof.\n829: Q:\n830: Who can vote at the special meeting?\n831: A:\n832: Holders of record of the Company’s preferred stock or common stock at the close of business on\n833: September 16, 2016 which is the record date for the special meeting, are entitled to notice of the special\n834: 14\n835: Table of Contents\n836: meeting and to vote at the special meeting. As of the close of business on the record date, there were 4,866,593 shares of common stock and 640 shares of preferred stock outstanding and entitled\n837: to vote at the special meeting. Each share of common stock and preferred stock is entitled to one vote per share on each matter properly brought before the special meeting. The common stock and the preferred stock are voting as separate classes on\n838: the proposals to be considered at the special meeting.\n839: Q:\n840: What constitutes a quorum for the special meeting?\n841: A.\n842: A quorum will be present if holders of record of a majority of the shares of preferred stock and a majority of the shares of common stock outstanding at the close of business on the record date are present in person or\n843: represented by proxy at the special meeting. If a quorum is not present at the special meeting, the special meeting may be adjourned or postponed from time to time until a quorum is obtained. As of the close of business on the record date, there\n844: were 4,866,593 shares of common stock and 640 shares of preferred stock outstanding. Accordingly, 2,433,297 shares of common stock and 321 shares of preferred stock must be present or represented by proxy at the special meeting to constitute a\n845: quorum.\n846: Q:\n847: What shareholder approvals are required to complete the merger?\n848: A:\n849: Approval of the merger agreement requires the affirmative vote of (1) holders of at least a majority of the shares of common stock outstanding at the close of business on the record date for the special meeting, and (2)\n850: holders of at least a majority of the shares of preferred stock outstanding at the close of business on the record date for the special meeting, voting as separate classes and not together. A failure to vote your shares of common stock or preferred\n851: stock or an abstention from voting will have the same effect as a vote “\n852: AGAINST\n853: ” the proposal to approve the merger agreement. Note that you may vote to approve the merger agreement and vote not to approve the non-binding\n854: compensation proposal or the adjournment proposal and vice versa.\n855: Q:\n856: What shareholder vote is required to approve the other proposals to be voted upon at the special meeting?\n857: A.\n858: Each of the non-binding compensation proposal and the adjournment proposal requires the affirmative vote of holders of a majority of the shares of common stock and a majority of the shares of preferred stock, each\n859: voting as separate classes and not together, cast for or against that proposal at the special meeting. Failures to vote and “broker non-votes” will have no effect on the outcome of the non-binding compensation proposal or adjournment\n860: proposal. However, abstentions will have the same effect as a vote “\n861: AGAINST\n862: ” the non-binding compensation proposal and the adjournment proposal. If less than a majority of the outstanding shares of common stock and\n863: preferred stock entitled to vote are present in person or represented by proxy at the special meeting, a majority of the shares of each class present or represented at the meeting, each voting as a separate class, may also adjourn the meeting under\n864: the Company’s bylaws.\n865: Q:\n866: Are there any shareholders already committed to voting in favor of the merger agreement?\n867: A:\n868: Yes. The Eder Trusts have entered into a voting agreement with G&W and the Company requiring them to vote all of their shares in favor of approval of the merger agreement. These shareholders collectively held\n869: approximately 78.1% of the outstanding shares of preferred stock and 17.3% of the outstanding shares of common stock on the record date.\n870: Q:\n871: Does the Board recommend voting in favor of the merger agreement?\n872: A:\n873: Yes. After careful consideration of various factors and after consulting with its outside legal counsel and financial advisor, the Board recommends that the Company’s shareholders vote\n874: “FOR”\n875: approval of the merger agreement. The Board also recommends that shareholders vote “\n876: FOR\n877: ” approval of the non-binding compensation proposal and “\n878: FOR\n879: ” the adjournment proposal.\n880: 15\n881: Table of Contents\n882: Q:\n883: Do any of the Company’s directors or executive officers have interests in the merger that may differ from those of the Company’s shareholders generally?\n884: A:\n885: Yes. In considering the recommendation of the Board with respect to the merger proposal, you should be aware that our directors and executive officers may have interests in the merger that are different from, or in\n886: addition to, the interests of the Company’s shareholders generally. During its consideration of the merger, the Board was aware of and considered there interests, as described in the section of this proxy statement titled “Proposal\n887: No.1—The Merger—Interests of Directors and Executive Officers in the Merger” beginning on page 44.\n888: Q:\n889: Why am I being asked to approve the non-binding compensation proposal?\n890: A:\n891: SEC rules require the Company to seek an advisory (non-binding) vote with respect to certain categories of compensation that may be provided to named executive officers in connection with a merger transaction.\n892: Q:\n893: What will happen if the shareholders do not approve the non-binding compensation proposal?\n894: A:\n895: Approval of the compensation that may be paid or become payable in connection with, or following completion of, the merger is not a condition to completion of the merger. The vote with respect to the compensation that\n896: may be paid or become payable is an advisory vote and will not be binding on the Company. Therefore, if shareholders approve the proposal to approve the merger agreement by the requisite majorities and the merger is completed, the payments that are\n897: the subject of the vote may become payable to the named executive officers regardless of the outcome of such vote.\n898: Q:\n899: What will happen if the merger is not completed?\n900: A:\n901: If the merger agreement is not approved by the Company’s shareholders or if the merger is not completed for any other reason, the Company’s shareholders will not receive any payment for their shares of common\n902: stock or preferred stock. Instead, the Company will remain an independent public company, the Company’s common stock will continue to be listed and traded on the NASDAQ and registered under the Exchange Act, and the Company will continue to\n903: file periodic reports with the SEC.\n904: Under specified circumstances, the Company may be required to pay G&W a\n905: termination fee of $3,785,000 upon the termination of the merger agreement, as described in the section of this proxy statement titled “The Merger Agreement—Termination Fee” beginning on page 55.\n906: Q:\n907: What will happen to my stock options and restricted stock unit awards under the Company’s Equity Plans?\n908: A:\n909: If the merger is completed, any stock option you hold, whether or not vested, which is outstanding immediately prior to the effective time of the merger and which has not been exercised or canceled, will be canceled in\n910: exchange for your right to receive a lump sum cash payment equal to the product of (a) the excess, if any, of (i) the $25.00 per share merger consideration, without interest, over (ii) the per share exercise price for such option and (b) the total\n911: number of shares of common stock underlying such option, less applicable taxes required to be withheld.\n912: If the merger is\n913: completed, any restricted stock unit award, whether vested or unvested, which is outstanding immediately prior to the effective time of the merger, will be canceled in exchange for the right to receive a lump sum cash payment equal to the product of\n914: (i) the $25.00 per share merger consideration, without interest, and (ii) the number of shares of common stock subject to such restricted stock unit award, less applicable taxes required to be withheld.\n915: Q:\n916: What if I participate in the Company’s Profit-Sharing Plan or Employee Stock Purchase Plan?\n917: A:\n918: If you are a participant in the Providence and Worcester Railroad Company Profit-Sharing Plan (which we refer to\n919: as the “Profit-Sharing Plan”), your proxy will serve as voting instructions for your shares of\n920: 16\n921: Table of Contents\n922: common stock held in the Profit-Sharing Plan as of the record date. Participants in the Profit-Sharing Plan may direct the trustee of the Profit-Sharing Plan as to how to vote shares allocated to\n923: their Profit-Sharing Plan account. The cutoff date for voting for participants in the Profit-Sharing Plan is the close of business on October 24, 2016. If you do not provide voting instructions, the trustee will not vote shares allocated to your\n924: Profit-Sharing Plan account.\n925: If you are a participant in the Providence and Worcester Railroad Company Employee Stock\n926: Purchase Plan (which we refer to as the “ESPP”), you should have received a proxy statement and proxy for shares of common stock purchased under the ESPP. Although these shares are uncertificated, they are registered on the Company’s\n927: books in your name as “book-entry shares” and you have the right to vote such shares to the same extent, and in the same manner, as any other shares that you own.\n928: See the section of this proxy statement titled “The Special Meeting of the Shareholders—Shares Held in the PWRR Profit-Sharing\n929: Plan” beginning on page 26 and the section titled “The Special Meeting of the Shareholders—Shares Purchased Under the PWRR Employee Stock Purchase Plan” beginning on page 26 of this proxy statement.\n930: Q:\n931: How may I vote my shares for the special meeting proposals presented in this proxy statement?\n932: A:\n933: You may vote by completing, signing, dating and returning the proxy card in the enclosed prepaid return envelope as soon as possible. This will enable your shares to be represented and voted at the special meeting.\n934: Proxy cards mailed by holders of record must be received prior to 10:00 a.m. on October 26, 2016, in order to be counted in the vote.\n935: Q:\n936: If my shares are held in “street name” by my broker, bank or other nominee, will my broker, bank or other nominee automatically vote my shares for me?\n937: A:\n938: No. Your broker, bank or other nominee\n939: will not\n940: vote your shares unless you provide instructions to your broker, bank or other nominee on how to vote. It is important that you provide timely instruction to\n941: your broker or bank to ensure that all shares of Company stock that you own are voted at the special meeting. You should fill out the voter instruction form sent to you by your broker, bank or other nominee with this proxy statement.\n942: Q:\n943: What if I fail to return my proxy card or to instruct my broker, bank or other nominee?\n944: A:\n945: If you fail to return your proxy card or to instruct your broker, bank or other nominee to vote your shares, your shares will not be voted. This will have the same effect as a vote “\n946: AGAINST\n947: ” approval of\n948: the merger agreement. Failures to vote and “broker non-votes” will have no effect on the outcome of the other proposals.\n949: Q:\n950: What do I need to do now?\n951: A:\n952: You should carefully read and consider the information contained or incorporated by reference into this proxy statement, including its annexes. It contains important information about the merger, the merger agreement,\n953: and the Company. After you have read and considered this information, shareholders are requested to vote by mail or by attending the special meeting and voting in person. If you choose to vote by mail, you should complete, sign and date your proxy\n954: card and return it in the enclosed postage-paid return envelope as soon as possible so that your shares of Company stock will be represented and voted at the special meeting. The proxy card will instruct the persons named on the proxy card to vote\n955: your shares at the special meeting as you direct. If you sign and send in a proxy card and do not indicate how you wish to vote, the proxy will be voted “\n956: FOR\n957: ” all of the special meeting proposals.\n958: 17\n959: Table of Contents\n960: Q.\n961: What should I do if I receive more than one set of voting materials?\n962: A.\n963: You may receive more than one set of voting materials, including multiple copies of this proxy statement and multiple proxy cards or voting instruction cards. For example, if you hold your shares in more than one\n964: brokerage account, you will receive a separate voting instruction card for each brokerage account in which you hold the shares. Please complete, sign, date and return each proxy card and voting instruction card that you receive, or otherwise follow\n965: the voting instructions set forth on the proxy card and voting instruction card.\n966: Q:\n967: Can I attend the special meeting and vote my shares in person?\n968: A:\n969: Yes. Although the Board requests that you vote your shares by mail in advance of the special meeting, all of the Company’s shareholders are invited to attend the special meeting. Shareholders of record on\n970: September 16, 2016 may vote in person at the special meeting. If your shares are held by a broker, bank or other nominee, then you are not the shareholder of record and you must bring to the special meeting appropriate documentation from your\n971: broker, bank or other nominee to enable you to vote at the special meeting.\n972: Q:\n973: Can I change my vote after I have submitted a proxy?\n974: A:\n975: Yes. If you have not voted through your broker, bank or other nominee, there are three ways you can change your vote at any time after you have sent in your proxy card and before your proxy is voted at the special\n976: meeting:\n977: •\n978: You may file a timely written revocation of the proxy with the Secretary and General Counsel of the Company, Charles D. Rennick, Providence and Worcester Railroad Company, 75 Hammond Street, Worcester, Massachusetts\n979: 01610;\n980: •\n981: You may timely submit a new signed proxy card bearing a later date (any earlier proxies will be revoked automatically); or\n982: •\n983: You may attend the special meeting and vote in person provided that you are the holder of record of your shares and have filed a written revocation of your grant of proxy with the Secretary and General Counsel of the\n984: Company as indicated above.\n985: If you have instructed a bank, broker or other nominee to vote your shares, you must follow the\n986: directions you receive from your bank, broker or other nominee to change your vote.\n987: Q:\n988: What happens if I sell my shares after the record date but before the special meeting?\n989: A:\n990: The record date of the special meeting is earlier than both the date of the special meeting and the date that the merger is expected to be completed. If you sell or otherwise transfer your Company shares after the\n991: record date but before the date of the special meeting, you will retain your right to vote at the special meeting unless special arrangements (such as provision of a proxy) are made between you and the person to whom you sell or otherwise transfer\n992: your shares and each of you notifies the Company in writing of such special arrangements, but you will transfer the right to receive the merger consideration if the merger is completed to the person to whom you transferred your shares. In order to\n993: receive the merger consideration, you must hold your shares through completion of the merger.\n994: Q:\n995: Am I entitled to exercise dissenters’ rights instead of receiving the merger consideration for my shares?\n996: A:\n997: Under Rhode Island corporate law, because the common stock is listed on the NASDAQ, holders of common stock who\n998: object to the merger do not have any appraisal or dissenters’ rights. Holders of preferred\n999: 18\n1000: Table of Contents\n1001: stock are entitled to certain dissenters’ rights under Rhode Island law as long as they follow the procedures precisely and satisfy certain conditions, which are described in the proxy\n1002: statement under the section titled “Dissenters’ Rights” beginning on page 68. Failure to strictly comply with the statutory requirements may result in your waiver of, or inability to exercise, dissenters’ rights.\n1003: Q:\n1004: Should I send in my stock certificates now?\n1005: A:\n1006: No. You will receive separate written instructions for surrendering your shares of common stock or preferred stock in exchange for the merger consideration. In the meantime, you should retain your stock certificate(s)\n1007: because they are still valid. Please do not send in your stock certificate(s) with your proxy card.\n1008: Q:\n1009: Whom should I call with questions?\n1010: A:\n1011: If you have questions about the merger or the special meeting, or if you need additional copies of this proxy statement or the enclosed proxy card, you should contact the Company’s Investor Relations Contact, Wendy\n1012: Lavely, at\n1013: (508) 755-4000, ext. 365.\n1014: Q:\n[Output truncated. Continue reading with offset: 1015]","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":true},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /tmp/opencode/filing_lines.txt, lines 735-1014\n735: Annex D\n736: and incorporated into this proxy statement by reference. Failure to timely comply with the procedures set forth in Section 7-1.2-1202 of the RIBCA\n737: will result in loss of a preferred shareholder’s dissenters’ rights.\n738: Market Price of Our Common Stock\n739: (page 71)\n740: Our common stock is listed on the NASDAQ under the trading symbol “PWX”. The closing sale price of Company common stock on\n741: the NASDAQ on August 12, 2016, which was the last trading day before we announced the merger, was $16.30. On September 16, 2016, the last trading day before the date of this proxy statement, the closing price of Company common stock on the NASDAQ\n742: was $24.88.\n743: 11\n744: Table of Contents\n745: Material Federal Income Tax Consequences\n746: (page 74)\n747: The receipt of cash for shares of Company stock pursuant to the merger generally will be a taxable transaction for United States federal income\n748: tax purposes. In general, if you are a U.S. Holder (as defined under “Material United States Federal Income Tax Consequences of the Merger”), you will recognize gain or loss for United States federal income tax purposes in an amount equal\n749: to the difference between the amount of cash received and your adjusted tax basis in your shares of common stock or preferred stock, as applicable. Generally, any gain recognized upon the exchange will be capital gain, and any such capital gain will\n750: be long-term capital gain if you have established a holding period of more than one year for your shares of Company stock.\n751: Please review\n752: the information under “Material United States Federal Income Tax Consequences of the Merger.” Tax matters are complicated, and the tax consequences of the merger to you will depend upon the facts of your particular situation. In addition,\n753: you may be subject to state, local or foreign tax laws that are not discussed in this document.\n754: Accordingly, we strongly urge you to consult your own tax advisor for a full understanding of the tax consequences to you of the merger.\n755: 12\n756: Table of Contents\n757: QUESTIONS AND ANSWERS ABOUT THE MERGER AND THE SPECIAL MEETING\n758: The following questions and answers are intended to address briefly some commonly asked questions regarding the merger and the\n759: special meeting. These questions and answers may not address all questions that may be important to you as a shareholder. To more fully understand the merger and the special meeting, you should read this entire proxy statement, including the\n760: materials attached as annexes, as well as the documents that have been incorporated by reference into this proxy statement.\n761: Q:\n762: Why am I receiving this proxy statement?\n763: A:\n764: G&W and the Company have agreed to the acquisition of the Company by G&W under the terms of a merger agreement that is described in this proxy statement. A copy of the merger agreement is attached to this proxy\n765: statement as\n766: Annex A\n767: . In order to complete the merger, the holders of a majority of preferred stock and the holders of a majority of common stock, each voting as a separate class, must approve the merger agreement. The Company will hold a\n768: special meeting of its shareholders to obtain these approvals. You are receiving this proxy statement in connection with the solicitation by the Board of proxies of the Company’s shareholders in favor of the merger proposal.\n769: You are also being asked to vote on a proposal to approve on an advisory (non-binding) basis the compensation that may be paid or become\n770: payable by the Company to the Company’s named executive officers in connection with, or following the completion of, the merger and on a proposal to approve an adjournment of the special meeting, if necessary, to permit further solicitation of\n771: proxies if there are not sufficient votes at the time of the special meeting to approve the merger agreement.\n772: This proxy statement\n773: contains important information about the merger, the merger agreement, the special meeting of the Company’s shareholders, and other related matters, and you should read it carefully. The enclosed voting materials for the special meeting allow\n774: you to vote your shares of preferred stock and/or shares of common stock without attending the special meeting. Your vote is very important. Even if you plan to attend the special meeting, we encourage you to submit a proxy as soon as possible.\n775: Q:\n776: What will happen in the merger?\n777: A:\n778: In the proposed merger, Merger Sub will merge with and into the Company, with the Company being the surviving corporation and becoming a wholly-owned subsidiary of G&W.\n779: Q:\n780: What will I receive in the merger?\n781: A:\n782: If the merger agreement is approved and the merger is subsequently completed, you will be entitled to receive $25.00 in cash, without interest, less any applicable withholding taxes, for each share of common stock you\n783: own. If the merger is completed, each outstanding share of preferred stock will be deemed to be automatically converted based upon its existing 100-for-1 conversion ratio, along with the aggregate accrued or accumulated and unpaid dividends thereon,\n784: into 100 shares of common stock, and each holder of preferred stock will be entitled to receive $25.00 in cash for each share of common stock into which the preferred stock has been deemed converted, without interest, less any applicable withholding\n785: taxes.\n786: For example, if you own 100 shares of common stock you will receive $2,500 in cash for your shares of common stock,\n787: less any applicable withholding taxes, and if you own 10 shares of preferred stock, your 10 shares of preferred stock will be deemed converted into 1,000 shares of common stock and you will receive $25,000 in cash in for your shares of preferred\n788: stock deemed converted into common stock, less any applicable withholding taxes. You will not own any shares of the capital stock in the surviving corporation.\n789: Q:\n790: What other effects will the merger have on the Company?\n791: A:\n792: If the merger is completed, the common stock will be delisted from the NASDAQ and deregistered under the Exchange\n793: Act, and the Company will no longer be required to file periodic reports with the SEC with\n794: 13\n795: Table of Contents\n796: respect to the common stock, in each case, in accordance with applicable law, rules and regulations. Following the completion of the merger, the common stock will no longer be publicly traded and\n797: you will no longer have any interest in the Company’s future earnings or growth.\n798: Q:\n799: What are the material federal income tax consequences of the merger to me?\n800: A:\n801: The receipt of cash for shares of Company stock pursuant to the merger generally will be a taxable transaction for United States federal income tax purposes. In general, if you are a U.S. Holder (as defined under\n802: “Material United States Federal Income Tax Consequences of the Merger”), you will recognize gain or loss for United States federal income tax purposes in an amount equal to the difference between the amount of cash received and your\n803: adjusted tax basis in your shares of common stock or preferred stock, as applicable. Generally, any gain recognized upon the exchange will be capital gain, and any such capital gain will be long-term capital gain if you have established a holding\n804: period of more than one year for your shares of Company stock.\n805: This tax treatment may not apply to all the Company’s\n806: shareholders. We strongly urge you to review the information under “Material United States Federal Income Tax Consequences of the Merger” and consult your own tax advisor for a full understanding of the tax consequences of the merger to\n807: you.\n808: Q:\n809: What are the conditions to completion of the merger?\n810: A:\n811: The obligations of G&W and the Company to complete the merger are subject to the satisfaction or waiver of certain closing conditions contained in the merger agreement, including the receipt of required regulatory\n812: approvals and approval of the merger agreement by a majority of the holders of preferred stock and the holders of common stock, each voting as a separate class.\n813: Q:\n814: When do you expect the merger to be completed?\n815: A:\n816: We will complete the merger when all of the conditions to completion contained in the merger agreement are satisfied or waived. Some of these conditions, such as the receipt of required regulatory approvals, are not\n817: entirely within our control. We currently expect to complete the merger during the fourth calendar quarter of 2016 assuming timely satisfaction of necessary closing conditions; however, because the merger is subject to these conditions, we cannot\n818: predict the actual timing and we cannot guarantee that the merger will be completed.\n819: Q:\n820: When and where is the special meeting?\n821: A:\n822: The special meeting of shareholders of the Company will be held at Union Station located at Two Washington Square, Worcester, Massachusetts 01604, on Wednesday, October 26, 2016 at 10:00 a.m., local time.\n823: Q:\n824: What will happen at the special meeting?\n825: A:\n826: At the special meeting, the Company’s shareholders will consider and vote upon a proposal to approve the merger agreement and the non-binding compensation proposal. If, at the time of the special meeting, there are\n827: not sufficient votes to approve the merger agreement, we may ask you to consider and vote upon the adjournment proposal, so that we can solicit additional proxies. Shareholders may also be asked to consider and act upon such other matters as may\n828: properly come before the special meeting or any adjournment or postponement thereof.\n829: Q:\n830: Who can vote at the special meeting?\n831: A:\n832: Holders of record of the Company’s preferred stock or common stock at the close of business on\n833: September 16, 2016 which is the record date for the special meeting, are entitled to notice of the special\n834: 14\n835: Table of Contents\n836: meeting and to vote at the special meeting. As of the close of business on the record date, there were 4,866,593 shares of common stock and 640 shares of preferred stock outstanding and entitled\n837: to vote at the special meeting. Each share of common stock and preferred stock is entitled to one vote per share on each matter properly brought before the special meeting. The common stock and the preferred stock are voting as separate classes on\n838: the proposals to be considered at the special meeting.\n839: Q:\n840: What constitutes a quorum for the special meeting?\n841: A.\n842: A quorum will be present if holders of record of a majority of the shares of preferred stock and a majority of the shares of common stock outstanding at the close of business on the record date are present in person or\n843: represented by proxy at the special meeting. If a quorum is not present at the special meeting, the special meeting may be adjourned or postponed from time to time until a quorum is obtained. As of the close of business on the record date, there\n844: were 4,866,593 shares of common stock and 640 shares of preferred stock outstanding. Accordingly, 2,433,297 shares of common stock and 321 shares of preferred stock must be present or represented by proxy at the special meeting to constitute a\n845: quorum.\n846: Q:\n847: What shareholder approvals are required to complete the merger?\n848: A:\n849: Approval of the merger agreement requires the affirmative vote of (1) holders of at least a majority of the shares of common stock outstanding at the close of business on the record date for the special meeting, and (2)\n850: holders of at least a majority of the shares of preferred stock outstanding at the close of business on the record date for the special meeting, voting as separate classes and not together. A failure to vote your shares of common stock or preferred\n851: stock or an abstention from voting will have the same effect as a vote “\n852: AGAINST\n853: ” the proposal to approve the merger agreement. Note that you may vote to approve the merger agreement and vote not to approve the non-binding\n854: compensation proposal or the adjournment proposal and vice versa.\n855: Q:\n856: What shareholder vote is required to approve the other proposals to be voted upon at the special meeting?\n857: A.\n858: Each of the non-binding compensation proposal and the adjournment proposal requires the affirmative vote of holders of a majority of the shares of common stock and a majority of the shares of preferred stock, each\n859: voting as separate classes and not together, cast for or against that proposal at the special meeting. Failures to vote and “broker non-votes” will have no effect on the outcome of the non-binding compensation proposal or adjournment\n860: proposal. However, abstentions will have the same effect as a vote “\n861: AGAINST\n862: ” the non-binding compensation proposal and the adjournment proposal. If less than a majority of the outstanding shares of common stock and\n863: preferred stock entitled to vote are present in person or represented by proxy at the special meeting, a majority of the shares of each class present or represented at the meeting, each voting as a separate class, may also adjourn the meeting under\n864: the Company’s bylaws.\n865: Q:\n866: Are there any shareholders already committed to voting in favor of the merger agreement?\n867: A:\n868: Yes. The Eder Trusts have entered into a voting agreement with G&W and the Company requiring them to vote all of their shares in favor of approval of the merger agreement. These shareholders collectively held\n869: approximately 78.1% of the outstanding shares of preferred stock and 17.3% of the outstanding shares of common stock on the record date.\n870: Q:\n871: Does the Board recommend voting in favor of the merger agreement?\n872: A:\n873: Yes. After careful consideration of various factors and after consulting with its outside legal counsel and financial advisor, the Board recommends that the Company’s shareholders vote\n874: “FOR”\n875: approval of the merger agreement. The Board also recommends that shareholders vote “\n876: FOR\n877: ” approval of the non-binding compensation proposal and “\n878: FOR\n879: ” the adjournment proposal.\n880: 15\n881: Table of Contents\n882: Q:\n883: Do any of the Company’s directors or executive officers have interests in the merger that may differ from those of the Company’s shareholders generally?\n884: A:\n885: Yes. In considering the recommendation of the Board with respect to the merger proposal, you should be aware that our directors and executive officers may have interests in the merger that are different from, or in\n886: addition to, the interests of the Company’s shareholders generally. During its consideration of the merger, the Board was aware of and considered there interests, as described in the section of this proxy statement titled “Proposal\n887: No.1—The Merger—Interests of Directors and Executive Officers in the Merger” beginning on page 44.\n888: Q:\n889: Why am I being asked to approve the non-binding compensation proposal?\n890: A:\n891: SEC rules require the Company to seek an advisory (non-binding) vote with respect to certain categories of compensation that may be provided to named executive officers in connection with a merger transaction.\n892: Q:\n893: What will happen if the shareholders do not approve the non-binding compensation proposal?\n894: A:\n895: Approval of the compensation that may be paid or become payable in connection with, or following completion of, the merger is not a condition to completion of the merger. The vote with respect to the compensation that\n896: may be paid or become payable is an advisory vote and will not be binding on the Company. Therefore, if shareholders approve the proposal to approve the merger agreement by the requisite majorities and the merger is completed, the payments that are\n897: the subject of the vote may become payable to the named executive officers regardless of the outcome of such vote.\n898: Q:\n899: What will happen if the merger is not completed?\n900: A:\n901: If the merger agreement is not approved by the Company’s shareholders or if the merger is not completed for any other reason, the Company’s shareholders will not receive any payment for their shares of common\n902: stock or preferred stock. Instead, the Company will remain an independent public company, the Company’s common stock will continue to be listed and traded on the NASDAQ and registered under the Exchange Act, and the Company will continue to\n903: file periodic reports with the SEC.\n904: Under specified circumstances, the Company may be required to pay G&W a\n905: termination fee of $3,785,000 upon the termination of the merger agreement, as described in the section of this proxy statement titled “The Merger Agreement—Termination Fee” beginning on page 55.\n906: Q:\n907: What will happen to my stock options and restricted stock unit awards under the Company’s Equity Plans?\n908: A:\n909: If the merger is completed, any stock option you hold, whether or not vested, which is outstanding immediately prior to the effective time of the merger and which has not been exercised or canceled, will be canceled in\n910: exchange for your right to receive a lump sum cash payment equal to the product of (a) the excess, if any, of (i) the $25.00 per share merger consideration, without interest, over (ii) the per share exercise price for such option and (b) the total\n911: number of shares of common stock underlying such option, less applicable taxes required to be withheld.\n912: If the merger is\n913: completed, any restricted stock unit award, whether vested or unvested, which is outstanding immediately prior to the effective time of the merger, will be canceled in exchange for the right to receive a lump sum cash payment equal to the product of\n914: (i) the $25.00 per share merger consideration, without interest, and (ii) the number of shares of common stock subject to such restricted stock unit award, less applicable taxes required to be withheld.\n915: Q:\n916: What if I participate in the Company’s Profit-Sharing Plan or Employee Stock Purchase Plan?\n917: A:\n918: If you are a participant in the Providence and Worcester Railroad Company Profit-Sharing Plan (which we refer to\n919: as the “Profit-Sharing Plan”), your proxy will serve as voting instructions for your shares of\n920: 16\n921: Table of Contents\n922: common stock held in the Profit-Sharing Plan as of the record date. Participants in the Profit-Sharing Plan may direct the trustee of the Profit-Sharing Plan as to how to vote shares allocated to\n923: their Profit-Sharing Plan account. The cutoff date for voting for participants in the Profit-Sharing Plan is the close of business on October 24, 2016. If you do not provide voting instructions, the trustee will not vote shares allocated to your\n924: Profit-Sharing Plan account.\n925: If you are a participant in the Providence and Worcester Railroad Company Employee Stock\n926: Purchase Plan (which we refer to as the “ESPP”), you should have received a proxy statement and proxy for shares of common stock purchased under the ESPP. Although these shares are uncertificated, they are registered on the Company’s\n927: books in your name as “book-entry shares” and you have the right to vote such shares to the same extent, and in the same manner, as any other shares that you own.\n928: See the section of this proxy statement titled “The Special Meeting of the Shareholders—Shares Held in the PWRR Profit-Sharing\n929: Plan” beginning on page 26 and the section titled “The Special Meeting of the Shareholders—Shares Purchased Under the PWRR Employee Stock Purchase Plan” beginning on page 26 of this proxy statement.\n930: Q:\n931: How may I vote my shares for the special meeting proposals presented in this proxy statement?\n932: A:\n933: You may vote by completing, signing, dating and returning the proxy card in the enclosed prepaid return envelope as soon as possible. This will enable your shares to be represented and voted at the special meeting.\n934: Proxy cards mailed by holders of record must be received prior to 10:00 a.m. on October 26, 2016, in order to be counted in the vote.\n935: Q:\n936: If my shares are held in “street name” by my broker, bank or other nominee, will my broker, bank or other nominee automatically vote my shares for me?\n937: A:\n938: No. Your broker, bank or other nominee\n939: will not\n940: vote your shares unless you provide instructions to your broker, bank or other nominee on how to vote. It is important that you provide timely instruction to\n941: your broker or bank to ensure that all shares of Company stock that you own are voted at the special meeting. You should fill out the voter instruction form sent to you by your broker, bank or other nominee with this proxy statement.\n942: Q:\n943: What if I fail to return my proxy card or to instruct my broker, bank or other nominee?\n944: A:\n945: If you fail to return your proxy card or to instruct your broker, bank or other nominee to vote your shares, your shares will not be voted. This will have the same effect as a vote “\n946: AGAINST\n947: ” approval of\n948: the merger agreement. Failures to vote and “broker non-votes” will have no effect on the outcome of the other proposals.\n949: Q:\n950: What do I need to do now?\n951: A:\n952: You should carefully read and consider the information contained or incorporated by reference into this proxy statement, including its annexes. It contains important information about the merger, the merger agreement,\n953: and the Company. After you have read and considered this information, shareholders are requested to vote by mail or by attending the special meeting and voting in person. If you choose to vote by mail, you should complete, sign and date your proxy\n954: card and return it in the enclosed postage-paid return envelope as soon as possible so that your shares of Company stock will be represented and voted at the special meeting. The proxy card will instruct the persons named on the proxy card to vote\n955: your shares at the special meeting as you direct. If you sign and send in a proxy card and do not indicate how you wish to vote, the proxy will be voted “\n956: FOR\n957: ” all of the special meeting proposals.\n958: 17\n959: Table of Contents\n960: Q.\n961: What should I do if I receive more than one set of voting materials?\n962: A.\n963: You may receive more than one set of voting materials, including multiple copies of this proxy statement and multiple proxy cards or voting instruction cards. For example, if you hold your shares in more than one\n964: brokerage account, you will receive a separate voting instruction card for each brokerage account in which you hold the shares. Please complete, sign, date and return each proxy card and voting instruction card that you receive, or otherwise follow\n965: the voting instructions set forth on the proxy card and voting instruction card.\n966: Q:\n967: Can I attend the special meeting and vote my shares in person?\n968: A:\n969: Yes. Although the Board requests that you vote your shares by mail in advance of the special meeting, all of the Company’s shareholders are invited to attend the special meeting. Shareholders of record on\n970: September 16, 2016 may vote in person at the special meeting. If your shares are held by a broker, bank or other nominee, then you are not the shareholder of record and you must bring to the special meeting appropriate documentation from your\n971: broker, bank or other nominee to enable you to vote at the special meeting.\n972: Q:\n973: Can I change my vote after I have submitted a proxy?\n974: A:\n975: Yes. If you have not voted through your broker, bank or other nominee, there are three ways you can change your vote at any time after you have sent in your proxy card and before your proxy is voted at the special\n976: meeting:\n977: •\n978: You may file a timely written revocation of the proxy with the Secretary and General Counsel of the Company, Charles D. Rennick, Providence and Worcester Railroad Company, 75 Hammond Street, Worcester, Massachusetts\n979: 01610;\n980: •\n981: You may timely submit a new signed proxy card bearing a later date (any earlier proxies will be revoked automatically); or\n982: •\n983: You may attend the special meeting and vote in person provided that you are the holder of record of your shares and have filed a written revocation of your grant of proxy with the Secretary and General Counsel of the\n984: Company as indicated above.\n985: If you have instructed a bank, broker or other nominee to vote your shares, you must follow the\n986: directions you receive from your bank, broker or other nominee to change your vote.\n987: Q:\n988: What happens if I sell my shares after the record date but before the special meeting?\n989: A:\n990: The record date of the special meeting is earlier than both the date of the special meeting and the date that the merger is expected to be completed. If you sell or otherwise transfer your Company shares after the\n991: record date but before the date of the special meeting, you will retain your right to vote at the special meeting unless special arrangements (such as provision of a proxy) are made between you and the person to whom you sell or otherwise transfer\n992: your shares and each of you notifies the Company in writing of such special arrangements, but you will transfer the right to receive the merger consideration if the merger is completed to the person to whom you transferred your shares. In order to\n993: receive the merger consideration, you must hold your shares through completion of the merger.\n994: Q:\n995: Am I entitled to exercise dissenters’ rights instead of receiving the merger consideration for my shares?\n996: A:\n997: Under Rhode Island corporate law, because the common stock is listed on the NASDAQ, holders of common stock who\n998: object to the merger do not have any appraisal or dissenters’ rights. Holders of preferred\n999: 18\n1000: Table of Contents\n1001: stock are entitled to certain dissenters’ rights under Rhode Island law as long as they follow the procedures precisely and satisfy certain conditions, which are described in the proxy\n1002: statement under the section titled “Dissenters’ Rights” beginning on page 68. Failure to strictly comply with the statutory requirements may result in your waiver of, or inability to exercise, dissenters’ rights.\n1003: Q:\n1004: Should I send in my stock certificates now?\n1005: A:\n1006: No. You will receive separate written instructions for surrendering your shares of common stock or preferred stock in exchange for the merger consideration. In the meantime, you should retain your stock certificate(s)\n1007: because they are still valid. Please do not send in your stock certificate(s) with your proxy card.\n1008: Q:\n1009: Whom should I call with questions?\n1010: A:\n1011: If you have questions about the merger or the special meeting, or if you need additional copies of this proxy statement or the enclosed proxy card, you should contact the Company’s Investor Relations Contact, Wendy\n1012: Lavely, at\n1013: (508) 755-4000, ext. 365.\n1014: Q:\n[Output truncated. Continue reading with offset: 1015]"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"limit\": 280, \"offset\": 735, \"path\": \"/tmp/opencode/filing_lines.txt\"}"},"time":{"start":1789824402665,"end":1789824402841}}}}
